  "worked_solution": "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\frac{x}{4}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\frac{1}{4}(x+4C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+4C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\frac{1}{4}(x+4C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\frac{x}{4}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13949463/assets/336c38a44a3a40fcaf4a226f61a9f7af2cc731674c1ccc6679b80d846d4ea657.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\frac{x}{4})$ by $0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ units to the left. Therefore\n\n$$\n4C=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\u27f9\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
